1
00:00:00,700 --> 00:00:02,879
Two documents have the same length and

2
00:00:02,899 --> 00:00:04,824
match the same query term.

3
00:00:04,987 --> 00:00:06,242
One mentions it once.

4
00:00:06,404 --> 00:00:08,819
The other mentions it twenty times.

5
00:00:08,995 --> 00:00:11,165
Should the second get twenty times as much

6
00:00:11,185 --> 00:00:14,142
credit? Hold rarity and length fixed while

7
00:00:14,162 --> 00:00:15,043
you decide.

8
00:00:17,462 --> 00:00:20,052
BM twenty-five applies diminishing

9
00:00:20,072 --> 00:00:23,827
returns. Repetition contributes evidence,

10
00:00:23,847 --> 00:00:26,984
but each additional occurrence adds less.

11
00:00:27,134 --> 00:00:29,691
This term-frequency saturation prevents

12
00:00:29,711 --> 00:00:31,972
the contribution from growing linearly

13
00:00:31,992 --> 00:00:32,938
forever.

14
00:00:33,815 --> 00:00:36,721
At average document length, with k one set

15
00:00:36,741 --> 00:00:39,401
to one point two, one occurrence gives a

16
00:00:39,421 --> 00:00:41,359
term-frequency factor of one.

17
00:00:41,522 --> 00:00:44,323
Two give one point three seven five.

18
00:00:44,498 --> 00:00:47,107
Twenty give about two point zero eight.

19
00:00:47,668 --> 00:00:49,735
The curve approaches two point two.

20
00:00:49,923 --> 00:00:51,372
Both axes are linear.

21
00:00:51,534 --> 00:00:54,130
We are plotting a frequency factor, not a

22
00:00:54,150 --> 00:00:56,294
final score or a probability.

23
00:00:56,457 --> 00:00:59,426
Multiply this factor by the term's IDF to

24
00:00:59,446 --> 00:01:01,075
obtain its contribution.

25
00:01:01,997 --> 00:01:05,102
The parameter k one changes the saturation

26
00:01:05,122 --> 00:01:07,666
curve. These curves use the same linear

27
00:01:07,686 --> 00:01:10,243
scales and average document length.

28
00:01:10,418 --> 00:01:12,628
Different settings change how repeated

29
00:01:12,648 --> 00:01:15,463
occurrences add evidence and the limiting

30
00:01:15,483 --> 00:01:17,227
value they approach.

31
00:01:17,780 --> 00:01:20,584
With k one at zero, every positive

32
00:01:20,604 --> 00:01:22,426
occurrence count has factor one.

33
00:01:22,614 --> 00:01:25,391
Presence matters; further repetition does

34
00:01:25,411 --> 00:01:28,741
not. A missing term still contributes

35
00:01:28,761 --> 00:01:31,383
zero. These settings are mathematical

36
00:01:31,403 --> 00:01:34,708
comparisons, not recommendations for every

37
00:01:34,728 --> 00:01:35,557
collection.

38
00:01:36,465 --> 00:01:39,168
Now fix the query term, its collection

39
00:01:39,188 --> 00:01:41,074
rarity and one occurrence.

40
00:01:41,211 --> 00:01:44,013
Compare a document of forty analysed terms

41
00:01:44,033 --> 00:01:45,700
with one of four hundred.

42
00:01:45,863 --> 00:01:48,136
The collection average is one hundred.

43
00:01:48,324 --> 00:01:50,959
With length normalisation enabled, the

44
00:01:50,979 --> 00:01:53,111
shorter example has the larger

45
00:01:53,131 --> 00:01:54,115
contribution.

46
00:01:54,673 --> 00:01:57,021
Length provides context for the frequency.

47
00:01:57,196 --> 00:01:59,212
This does not say that short papers are

48
00:01:59,232 --> 00:02:01,738
always better. Other matching terms

49
00:02:01,758 --> 00:02:04,354
contribute too, and usefulness still

50
00:02:04,374 --> 00:02:06,364
depends on the reader's information need.

51
00:02:07,276 --> 00:02:09,717
The parameter b controls the strength of

52
00:02:09,737 --> 00:02:11,327
length normalisation.

53
00:02:11,503 --> 00:02:13,931
In this calculation, k one is one point

54
00:02:13,951 --> 00:02:17,410
two and b is zero point seven five.

55
00:02:17,585 --> 00:02:19,627
The two documents receive different

56
00:02:19,647 --> 00:02:21,121
frequency factors.

57
00:02:21,690 --> 00:02:25,183
Set b to zero and, with frequency held

58
00:02:25,203 --> 00:02:28,056
fixed, the two factors are equal.

59
00:02:28,205 --> 00:02:30,544
We have turned off the length effect, not

60
00:02:30,564 --> 00:02:31,587
the whole score.

61
00:02:31,775 --> 00:02:33,031
The figures are computed from an

62
00:02:33,051 --> 00:02:36,162
explicitly labeled single-field BM

63
00:02:36,182 --> 00:02:37,566
twenty-five variant.

64
00:02:38,470 --> 00:02:39,736
Keep the roles separate.

65
00:02:39,898 --> 00:02:42,480
IDF weights collection rarity.

66
00:02:42,656 --> 00:02:45,354
k one shapes repetition's contribution.

67
00:02:45,529 --> 00:02:47,983
b controls length normalisation.

68
00:02:48,145 --> 00:02:51,141
One point two and zero point seven five

69
00:02:51,161 --> 00:02:53,241
are typical defaults used in the book's

70
00:02:53,261 --> 00:02:54,968
worked example; products and

71
00:02:54,988 --> 00:02:56,785
configurations can differ.

72
00:02:57,317 --> 00:03:00,342
BM twenty-five combines measured lexical

73
00:03:00,362 --> 00:03:02,829
evidence. Its score does not tell you that

74
00:03:02,849 --> 00:03:04,646
the source answers your question.

75
00:03:04,821 --> 00:03:07,082
The final film follows those calculations

76
00:03:07,102 --> 00:03:09,543
through the same inverted index we built

77
00:03:09,563 --> 00:03:10,702
